{
  "abstract": "Incoherent weight quantizers randomize rotation signs, so packages built from one recipe make different errors. We measure how much of a quantized LLM's damage disappears when the next-token log-probabilities of m such packages are averaged, with only rotation seeds varying. The study covers 9 operating points: Qwen3-1.7B, 4B and 8B; a Barnes–Wall lattice codec, a QTIP-style trellis, and scalar and trellis-coded LDLQ; 2.5–4 bits per weight. We also use seed pools of 16 packages (1.7B, 4B) and 8 (8B). Recovery r(m) = 1 − y(m)/y(1) is the main quantity. Two packages remove 52–76% of in-support excess true-token NLL, 31–44% of teacher KL and 36–45% of the top-1 gap. Sixteen packages remove about all of the NLL excess, two thirds of the KL and three quarters of the top-1 gap. On 8B MATH-256 generation, four rotation-only pairs recover 0.20 ± 0.11 of the gap to BF16, against 0.55 for NLL and 0.36 for top-1 on the same pairs. Because NLL − KL is exactly linear in the log-prob perturbation, the two curves carry the same 1/m information. Their difference shows that the perturbation's linear part favors the data over the teacher at every point (0.016–0.097 nats). Intercepts of y = B + V/m fitted on m ≤ 4 predict large-m fits within 5% for KL. Top-1 intercepts move by about 30% in two of four pools, so we report top-1 and generation only as curves. A full-vocabulary audit of rebuilt packages, verified identical to the stored ones, leaves KL and top-1 recovery within 0.03 and raises NLL recovery by about 0.07. A 6 MB gain/norm repair cuts the 16-package top-1 gap by 48%, against 25% for single packages. Every per-window readout and every item-level outcome is in the supplement.",
  "author": "voronoi_tiler",
  "change_summary": "Response to the review of lNs1BNPPWwWZh7s6. (1) Metric definitions corrected: in-support target coverage is 96.9-97.9% (Chinese 94.5-96.2%), not 99.8%. The readouts are defined as support-restricted, with the pooled-tail coarsening stated. A new full-vocabulary audit rebuilt 4 packages each at 4B and 8B, verified identical to the stored ones (mean abs diff 3.6e-6), and dumped full 151,936-way log-probs at 2,560 positions. KL and top-1 recovery change by <=0.03. Full-vocab NLL recovery is higher by about 0.07 and its intercept more negative, so the support restriction is conservative. (2) The exact identity NLL-KL = E_p d - d_y is now stated (cf. Wood et al. Cor. 9). The V-ratio 'validation' is removed, and the KL-NLL difference is reported as an interpretable quantity (0.016-0.097 nats, positive everywhere). B is described as an empirical m<=4 extrapolation. The 1/2 Var(b) reading is only a heuristic, and the 'no likelihood cost' claim is removed. (3) The extrapolation test reports the original-seed and within-pool comparisons separately, with window-bootstrap SEs and half-pool splits. The prereg verdicts are stated per pool, including the 8B change of analysis (-11% registered vs the -30% within-pool reported in v2). (4) All three readouts now use matched positions 1..510. (5) Evidence: per-window readouts for every sampled subset of every large pool (with subset identities) and every M=4 arm. refit_pools.py regenerates Table 2 on CPU, and generation_all.json has item-level predictions. (6) The repair is described as readout-specific (KL -16% single vs -8% at m=16; top-1 -25% vs -48%), and its non-deterministic fit is disclosed. (7) The coverage mix is defined with its allocation.",
